1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,650
A buyer files a chargeback.

2
00:00:02,650 --> 00:00:05,450
The seller has days to answer.

3
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:09,450
Miss it, and PayPal rules for the buyer.

4
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:13,550
CaseClerk answers them for small PayPal sellers.

5
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:17,500
Here it is, live on PayPal's sandbox.

6
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:22,750
One click opens a real sandbox chargeback on a test order.

7
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:29,950
Three minutes later, PayPal's webhook arrives, and CaseClerk checks its signature.

8
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:37,050
A Claude agent reads the order and the tracking, then stops to ask the seller.

9
00:00:37,700 --> 00:00:44,650
A harder one: a $412.00 camera the buyer says isn't as described.

10
00:00:44,900 --> 00:00:47,750
Tracking proves delivery, not condition.

11
00:00:47,750 --> 00:00:50,850
It estimates a 35% chance.

12
00:00:51,100 --> 00:00:55,650
It still recommends filing, because the expected loss is smaller.

13
00:00:55,900 --> 00:01:03,450
Over the $250 line, it stops and asks, with the evidence already written.

14
00:01:04,700 --> 00:01:06,450
The seller decides.

15
00:01:06,450 --> 00:01:10,450
A yes files it, with five seconds to undo.

16
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:18,650
CaseClerk files through PayPal's Disputes API.

17
00:01:18,650 --> 00:01:22,400
PayPal returns success and its own debug ID.

18
00:01:22,900 --> 00:01:27,350
We asked PayPal's sandbox to rule for the seller.

19
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:35,350
It ruled for the seller and released the hold: $388.18 kept.

20
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:41,350
This amount comes from PayPal's own hold and release records.

21
00:01:42,700 --> 00:01:48,600
You can ask the sandbox to rule on your own test case, too.

22
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:53,850
The seller's limits are enforced in code, not a prompt.

23
00:01:54,300 --> 00:02:02,250
Evidence under $250 and refunds up to $15 go out on their own.

24
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:05,850
Anything bigger waits for a yes.

25
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:12,150
Under the hood, every PayPal webhook is verified first.

26
00:02:12,300 --> 00:02:14,700
Claude Haiku sorts the case.

27
00:02:14,700 --> 00:02:19,650
Claude Opus works it with tools, and writes the evidence.

28
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:26,950
Every action is checked against those limits before reaching PayPal, and AI spend is capped.

29
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:32,450
Real sandbox chargebacks have run this whole loop, every answer kept.

30
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:40,950
For a one-person shop, every dispute gets an answer with evidence ready, not a loss by default.

31
00:02:41,100 --> 00:02:42,000
CaseClerk.

32
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:47,050
It answers your PayPal disputes, within the limits you set.
